Title: The Drift: Miles to Go
Episode: 101 - "Cold Chain"
Author: OpenHaul Creative & Media Collective
Format: Fountain Screenplay

EXT. PACIFIC COAST HIGHWAY - BIG SUR - DUSK

Rain hammers the cracked asphalt of Highway 1. Ocean waves smash violently against the jagged cliffs four hundred feet below. 

A lone 2021 high-roof Mercedes Sprinter van—battered, gray, customized with auxiliary roof antennas, external diesel heater exhaust, and heavy all-terrain tires—cuts through dense fog.

INT. SPRINTER VAN - CABIN - CONTINUOUS

LEO CARTER (38) grips the leather-wrapped steering wheel. Weathered hands, cuticles stained with mechanical grease, wedding ring worn thin. He wears a faded canvas jacket over a dark thermal henley.

On the custom center dashboard mount, an iPad ruggedized in a military-spec case displays the OPENHAUL APP:

SCREEN UI:
>> STATE: IN_CUSTODY_RFID_VERIFIED
>> MANIFEST #OH-88219-MED
>> PAYLOAD: PEDIATRIC INSULIN & WHOLE BLOOD UNITS (COLD-CHAIN)
>> TEMP SENSORS: 3.4°C [SAFE: 2.0°C - 8.0°C]
>> CELLULAR SIGNAL: NONE (0 BARS)
>> MESH PROTOCOL: P2P STMP ACTIVE [3 PEERS IN RANGE]

Beside the iPad, a small handheld UHF RFID scanner rests in a 3D-printed cradle, its green LED pulsing in rhythm with the cabin's ambient heater.

LEO
(muttering to himself)
Come on, sweet girl. Hold the line. Forty miles to Monterey.

Through the windshield, a FLASH OF LIGHTNING illuminates a mudslide warning sign hanging sideways off a guardrail. 

Behind Leo's seat, behind the steel partition, a LOW-PITCHED HUM vibrates through the chassis: the van's dedicated secondary lithium bank powering the mobile Norcold medical refrigeration cooler.

Suddenly—BEEP-BEEP-BEEP.

The iPad screen flashes AMBER:

>> WARNING: THERMAL EXCURSION ALERT
>> CARGO TEMP: 6.8°C (RISING: +0.4°C / MIN)
>> COMPRESSOR VOLTAGE DROP: 11.2V

Leo's eyes widen. He glances into his rearview mirror. 

LEO
No. Not today.

EXT. PCH TURNOUT - MOMENTS LATER

The van screeches to a stop in a gravel turnaround carved into the cliffside. Hazard lights amber-strobe the torrential rain.

Leo kicks the driver door open, throwing on a hooded slicker, and bounds out into the deluge.

INT. SPRINTER VAN - CARGO BAY - CONTINUOUS

Leo slides open the heavy side door. The air inside smells of ozone, neoprene, and cold steel.

In the center of the bay, strapped down with heavy ratchet webbing, sits a stainless-steel medical transport pod. 

A digital thermometer on the pod reads: 7.1°C.

Leo drops to his knees, ripping off his neoprene work gloves. He grabs an Allen wrench from a magnetic tool wall and pops the electrical access panel off the auxiliary lithium battery rack.

A 200-amp ANL fuse is glowing cherry red—nearly melted through from a loose terminal vibration.

LEO
Vibration shook the lug loose. 

He pulls an insulated ratchet and a spare gold-plated fuse from his vest. His hands are soaked, rain dripping from his eyelashes. 

EXT. HIGHWAY 1 - CONTINUOUS

Wind howls, shaking the entire van on its suspension. Small rocks tumble down the cliffside, clattering across the van's roof.

INT. SPRINTER VAN - CARGO BAY - CONTINUOUS

Leo wrenches the melted fuse free with a sharp SNAP. Sparks kick off the contact. 

He slams the fresh fuse into the busbar, tightens the lug with three deliberate, torqued turns.

CLACK.

The auxiliary inverter kicks back to life. A high-pitched fan whirrs. The refrigeration compressor thuds into gear.

On the transport pod, the temperature display stabilizes: 6.9°C... 6.5°C... 5.8°C... 4.2°C.

Leo exhales a heavy breath of steam. 

He picks up his handheld scanner, aims it at the pod's UHF RFID tag, and pulls the trigger.

BEEP.

The scanner display reads:
>> RFID HASH MATCH: 0x9FA2...77C
>> ZK-PROOF GENERATED: CUSTODY UNBROKEN
>> TEMP SLA PRESERVED

Leo taps the iPad on his forearm bracket:
>> DISPATCH BROADCAST VIA STMP MESH:
>> MONTEREY CLINIC INFORMED: ETA 34 MIN

INT. SPRINTER VAN - CABIN - MOMENTS LATER

Leo climbs back behind the wheel, soaked, wiping rain from his face with a towel. 

He puts the Sprinter into drive. The tires grip the wet gravel and pull back onto Highway 1, charging forward into the stormy night.

DISSOLVE TO:

EXT. MONTEREY COUNTY EMERGENCY CLINIC - NIGHT

A line of storm-displaced families waits beneath a temporary FEMA canvas awning in the parking lot. The clinic's emergency generator hums under a tarp.

DR. ELENA VANCE (45, scrubs under a yellow rain poncho) stands by the clinic receiving dock, checking her tablet with a look of quiet exhaustion.

Through the rain, twin headlights cut through the darkness. Leo's Sprinter rolls up to the dock and stops.

Leo jumps down, walks to the rear cargo doors, and swings them wide open.

DR. VANCE
Tell me you've got the cold units, Leo. The distributor in Salinas called it off six hours ago. Said the road was impassable.

LEO
Salinas doesn't drive a Sprinter.

Leo reaches inside and slides out the sealed stainless-steel transport pod. 

Elena raises her own clinic smartphone. The two devices chirp simultaneously as the cryptographic Bluetooth handshake completes.

CLINIC PHONE SCREEN:
>> OPENHAUL PROTOCOL HANDSHAKE VERIFIED
>> PROOF: ZK-SNARK #7701-TEMP-VALIDATED
>> IN_CUSTODY TRANSFERRED -> MONTEREY CLINIC
>> DRIVER PAYOUT ESCROW RELEASED: $148.50

Dr. Vance inspects the cold pod's tamper seal. She looks up at Leo, her face breaking into profound relief.

DR. VANCE
Unbroken cold chain. You saved sixty kids tonight, Leo.

Leo smiles faintly, nodding with humble respect.

LEO
Just doing my run, Doc. Just doing my run.

He closes the rear van doors with a solid THUD. 

Leo steps back into the cab, connects his seatbelt, and taps the OpenHaul app.

APP SCREEN:
>> STATUS: IDLE
>> NEW BROADCAST DISPATCH: FEMA RELIEF POD #99 - BIG SUR FIRE STATION
>> ACCEPT? [YES]

Leo taps [YES]. The engine roars. The van pulls away into the rain.

FADE OUT.
